{"builder":"Goal: when a config file has malformed JSON, the app's `bad config:` error must name the file path and the line and column of the mistake. `loadConfig` must keep throwing, and valid configs must load exactly as before. Do NOT do what the request suggested and catch the error and return `{}` or the defaults. That hides the typo and breaks the README's documented `bad config: <message>` and exit 1.\n\nCurrent code: `src/config.js` `loadConfig(path)` returns `JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'))`. `src/app.js` catches any error, writes `bad config: ${error.message}` to stderr and exits 1. `test/config.test.js` has two tests run by `npm test` (`node --test`).\n\nSteps:\n1. In `src/config.js`, read the file text, then call `JSON.parse` inside a try/catch that only wraps the parse. Leave read errors such as ENOENT as they are, because app.js already reports them as an exit-1 `bad config:` failure.\n2. On a parse error, throw a new error whose `message` contains the path exactly as it was passed in (do not resolve it to an absolute path) and the line and column of the mistake, for example `<path>:3:3: <original message>` or `<path> (line 3 column 3): ...`. Work out the line and column yourself; don't rely only on Node's message, since other Node versions may print only `at position N` or no location at all. Take the position from `/position (\\d+)/` in the original message, or from `line L column C` if present, then count the newlines in the text before that position: line is 1-based, and column is 1-based from the last newline. If no position can be found, still include the path. Keep the thrown error a `SyntaxError` (the existing test `bad JSON throws` asserts that), and set `cause` to the original error.\n3. Check by hand on Node v22: a file whose four lines are `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}` must make `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json` exit 1 with empty stdout. Its stderr must contain `bad config:`, `<tmp>/c.json` as typed, and line 3 and column 3. A file `{\"port\": 9000}` must still print `listening on 9000`, exit 0, and `loadConfig` must return `{ port: 9000 }`.\n4. Do not change `src/app.js` unless you need to. Its `bad config: ${error.message}` line with exit 1 is already right once the message carries the path and location.\n5. Add tests to `test/config.test.js`. At least one must fail on the current code: for a malformed file (for example the four-line file above, or `{port: 9000`), assert that `loadConfig` throws and that the error `message` includes the file path, and also that it includes the line and column (3 and 3 for the four-line file). Keep the existing tests passing. Optionally add a test that runs `src/app.js` as a child process and checks the exit code, stdout and stderr.\n6. Run `npm test` and make sure it passes. You may update the README to say the message names the file and location, but keep its `bad config: <message>` and exit 1 wording valid.\n\nNon-goals: no lenient formats (JSON5 or comments), no schema or value validation, and no change to how a missing file is reported beyond keeping it an exit-1 `bad config:` failure.","verifier":"Run every check at the PR head from the repository root, on Node v22 (`node --version`). Each check below matches one expect line, in the same order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/c.json`, where c.json contains the four lines `{`, `  \"port\": 9000,`, `  greeting: \"x\"`, `}`, exits with code 1 and prints nothing on stdout (in particular no `listening on` line).\"\nRun:\nd=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\\n  \"port\": 9000,\\n  greeting: \"x\"\\n}\\n' > \"$d/c.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/c.json\" >\"$d/out\" 2>\"$d/err\"; echo \"exit $?\"; echo \"stdout:\"; cat \"$d/out\"; echo \"stderr:\"; cat \"$d/err\"; echo \"path: $d/c.json\"\nPASS: it prints `exit 1` and nothing between `stdout:` and `stderr:`. FAIL: any other exit code, or any stdout output (such as `listening on`).\n\nCheck 2. Expect: \"In that same run, stderr contains `bad config:`, the full path `<tmp>/c.json` exactly as passed on the command line, the line number `3` and the column number `3` of the mistake.\"\nUse the output of the Check 1 command (the text after `stderr:`, and the path printed after `path:`).\nPASS: stderr contains `bad config:`, the exact path string printed after `path:`, and gives line 3 and column 3 of the mistake (for example `line 3 column 3`, `3:3` or `line 3, col 3`). FAIL: any of these is missing, the path is altered (for example a different path, or only the basename), or the line or column is not 3. Note: on the current code, Node v22's own message already includes `(line 3 column 3)` but no path.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: \"Calling `loadConfig(path)` on a file containing `{port: 9000` throws instead of returning a value, and the thrown error's `message` contains `path`.\"\nRun:\nd=$(mktemp -d); printf '{port: 9000' > \"$d/b.json\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; try { const v = loadConfig('$d/b.json'); console.log('RETURNED', JSON.stringify(v)); process.exit(2) } catch (e) { console.log(e.message.includes('$d/b.json') ? 'OK' : 'NO PATH: ' + e.message) }\"\nPASS: it prints `OK`. FAIL: it prints `RETURNED ...` (exit 2) or `NO PATH: ...`. On the current code it prints `NO PATH: ...`.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: \"Running `node src/app.js <tmp>/ok.json`, where ok.json contains `{\\\"port\\\": 9000}`, exits 0 and prints `listening on 9000` on stdout. `loadConfig` on that file returns `{ port: 9000 }`.\"\nRun:\nd=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"port\": 9000}' > \"$d/ok.json\"; node src/app.js \"$d/ok.json\"; echo \"exit $?\"; node --input-type=module -e \"import {loadConfig} from './src/config.js'; import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; const v = loadConfig('$d/ok.json'); assert.deepEqual(v, { port: 9000 }); console.log('OK', JSON.stringify(v))\"\nPASS: output is `listening on 9000`, then `exit 0`, then `OK {\"port\":9000}`. FAIL: a different stdout line, a non-zero exit, or an assertion error.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: \"`npm test` passes, and the test suite has at least one test that fails at the current base commit and checks that the error from malformed config JSON includes the file path.\"\nRun `npm test 2>&1 | tail -12; echo \"npm exit ${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"` at the PR head; it must show `# fail 0` and `npm exit 0`. Then set BASE to the PR's base commit (for example `BASE=$(git merge-base HEAD <the PR's target ref>)`), put the base's src/ under the PR's tests, run them, and restore:\ngit checkout \"$BASE\" -- src && npm test 2>&1 | grep -E '^# (pass|fail)|^not ok'; git checkout HEAD -- src && git status --short\nRead the failing tests' code in test/.\nPASS: `npm test` passes at the head, AND the base-src run shows at least one `not ok` test that asserts the malformed-JSON error message includes the file path. FAIL: `npm test` fails at the head, or no test fails against the base src/, or none of the tests that fail checks the path in the error.\n\nAdvisory (not pass or fail conditions):\n- Read `src/config.js` to see whether the line and column are computed from the text or position rather than taken only from Node's message. Node versions other than v22 may not print `line N column M`, so relying on Node's message alone is fragile.\n- Check that `loadConfig` does not return `{}` or the defaults for malformed JSON, that a missing file still gives an exit-1 `bad config:`, and that the path is not resolved to an absolute path.\n- The existing `bad JSON throws` test expects a `SyntaxError`; note it if the PR changed the error type or removed that test."}